URL Shortener
=============

Takes a long URL, returns a short code. Visit the code, get redirected.

Built to learn system design. Every change below fixes a problem found by
measuring, not guessing. The build log is the point of this repo.

Python, Flask, gunicorn, PostgreSQL 17, Redis 7, nginx, Docker Compose.

Run it
------

    cp .env.example .env            # then set your own values
    docker compose up -d --build --scale app=3
    docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U mia -d links < schema.sql

UI at http://127.0.0.1:8080. Or POST JSON to /shorten with a "url" field.

v1: in memory
-------------

Two endpoints, a Python dict, no database.

Problem: codes collide. token_urlsafe(4) gives 2^32 = 4.29 billion codes. At
10 million links that is 0.23% each, so ~23,000 links silently pointing at
the wrong site, with no error.

Longer codes only reduce the odds. Checking gives a guarantee, and writes are
rare, so paying on the write path is cheap.

Fix: retry on collision. The primary key does the checking.

v2: it survives a restart
-------------------------

Problem: the dict died on every restart.

Fix: SQLite. Code is the primary key, because every redirect arrives with a
code and needs the URL, never the reverse. Long URLs are not unique and must
not be: two people shortening google.com need different codes.

Then: is the disk read slow enough to need a cache? Isolated it with /ping, a
route touching no database. 856 req/sec against 817. Four percent apart, so
SQLite was not the bottleneck. The dev server was.

Fix: gunicorn, 4 workers. 817 to 6670 req/sec, not one line of app.py changed.
WSGI is a standard interface, so servers swap freely.

Cache decision: no. The database cost 0.22ms of a 1.50ms request.

v3: it can run on more than one machine
---------------------------------------

Problem: SQLite coordinates writers with operating system file locks, which
only works while every process asks the same operating system. On a network
share, two machines each believe they hold the lock. Both write. The file
corrupts, silently.

    SQLite     many processes, one machine
    Postgres   many processes, many machines

Fix: Postgres. Four changes, the SQL unchanged.

    sqlite3.connect("links.db")  ->  psycopg.connect(DATABASE_URL)
    ?                            ->  %s
    sqlite3.IntegrityError       ->  psycopg.errors.UniqueViolation

Cost: the database went from 0.22ms to 10.9ms. Fifty times more, same query.

The query or the connection? psycopg.connect() sat inside the function, so
every request paid a TCP handshake plus authentication for a 1ms query.

Fix: a connection pool, opened once at startup. 825 to 3759 req/sec for moving
one line out of a function. No new service.

v4: the cache
-------------

The database was now 1.39ms of a 2.66ms request. Added Redis to learn the
pattern, not because the numbers demanded it.

Cache-aside: check Redis, on a miss query Postgres and store the answer. The
cache knows nothing about the database. The app does the work.

3759 to 5386 req/sec. 43%, not the 2x expected. Redis is also a separate
process reached over TCP, so a Postgres trip became a shorter Redis one. A
cache gives a shorter trip, not memory speed.

Caveat: ab hits one URL repeatedly, so that was a 100% hit rate. A miss is
slower than no cache, since it checks Redis and still goes to Postgres.

Entries expire after an hour. That bounds staleness. It is not deletion.

v5: more than one copy
----------------------

No app code changed. All infrastructure.

Problem: --scale app=3 fails. Three containers, one host port.

    Bind for 0.0.0.0:5001 failed: port is already allocated

Publishing three ports would "work", but a short link is a URL with one host.
Whoever creates the link picks an address once, and every click lands on that
copy. The thing choosing has to choose per request, not once.

Fix: stop publishing the app's port, put nginx in front on 8080. Docker's DNS
returns all three IPs in rotating order, so the balancing is Docker's.

Gotcha: nginx resolves an upstream name once at startup and caches it forever.
A variable forces resolution per request.

    set $backend http://app:5001;
    proxy_pass $backend;

12 requests spread 3 / 3 / 6. DNS round robin is approximate.

What broke: nothing. State moved to Postgres in v3, so all three copies agree.
The v1 dict would have 404'd every redirect landing on a different copy.

What changed: the app sees nginx's IP, not the client's. Click analytics by
country would report one country. The fix is X-Forwarded-For.

Results
=======

ab -n 1000 -c 10, one laptop.

                                       req/sec    per req
    dev server + sqlite                    817     12.20ms
    gunicorn + sqlite                     6670      1.50ms
    gunicorn + postgres                    825     12.13ms
    gunicorn + postgres + pool            3759      2.66ms
    gunicorn + postgres + pool + redis    5386      1.90ms
    /ping, the floor                      7855      1.27ms

What this taught me
===================

* Measure before optimising. Twice I was sure the database was slow. Twice it
  was not. One fix was a word in a command, the other moved one line.
* To price something, add a route that does none of it and measure the gap.
* Slow is relative to the work, not to a second. 150ms across continents is
  fine. 11ms to return "ok" locally is not.
* Watch p99. The average hides your worst users.
* Fix the biggest bottleneck and the next becomes visible. Always was there.
* A cache is a decision with a price, not a default.
* Whatever all your copies must share becomes your ceiling.
* Check Non-2xx before believing a load test. ab reports zero failures for a
  thousand consecutive 404s.

Still to do
===========

* Cache invalidation, once links can be deleted
* X-Forwarded-For, so click analytics see real client IPs
